  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Supporting Regularization through the Implementation of Costa Rica&apos;s Temporary Special Migration Category</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Costa Rica has become the fourth country with the world&apos;s largest new individual asylum applications, with asylum seekers facing wait times of up to ten years to have their cases resolved due to COVID-19-related processing delays. In response, the Directorate General of Migration and Foreigners (DGME) in Costa Rica has opened a &#x201c;Temporary Special Category for Nationals of Cuba, Nicaragua, and Venezuela, whose asylum claims are pending resolution or have been denied&#x201d; to provide an alternative route to regularization. The DGME estimates that around 220,000 people will be able to have their cases regularized under this category by the end of 2023. The intervention aims to decrease the waiting period for eligible asylum seekers while providing a means for migrants who may not qualify for international protection to regularize their status in the country, increasing their access to services and integration opportunities, and decreasing their vulnerability. To ensure effective implementation, the International Organization for Migration (IOM) will provide technical and material support to DGME.</narrative>
  </description>
